WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:05.125
(warm tones resound)

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.070
- [Michael] It's important
to understand the struggle

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.060
because the truth is we're
always in the struggle.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:14.961
- [Crew Member] Marker.

00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:16.390
(clapperboard claps)

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:18.063
- Cincinnati in the '90s.

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:21.957
It's a hell of a decade,
Cincinnati in the '90s.

00:00:21.957 --> 00:00:24.220
- [Man] The very
foundations of our society

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.370
are in danger of being burned.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.580
- [Protestor] We're sick
and tired of being abused,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.680
and beaten, and bashed, and killed.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.907
- We refuse to be quiet.

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:38.400
- [Protestors] We are
everywhere, we are everywhere!

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.690
- [Man] Homosexuality is
incompatible with military service.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.993
- This is a human rights issue.

00:00:45.677 --> 00:00:49.117
- He said, "I have a dream today,"

00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.860
and when he spoke about
the sons of former slaves

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.460
and the sons of former slave owners

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.200
he made no distinction
between the straight ones

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.163
and the gay ones.
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.460
- [Man] What was the impact
as you start to, you know,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.860
find out about sexual
orientation protections in 1992?

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.240
Because it wasn't something
that was done with fanfare.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.330
You know, it was--
- Let's tell each other

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.400
the truth here though.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.433
I mean really, you wanna talk truth?

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.770
There were guys who were
very powerful in Procter,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.906
and only got more
powerful, who didn't want

00:01:27.906 --> 00:01:31.303
to give equal right to
the LGBTQ community.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.043
We didn't deserve it.

00:01:34.043 --> 00:01:36.880
(soft instrumental music)

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.880
- So moving to Cincinnati
was a challenge for me.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.520
I really wanted to help a
city like Cincinnati change,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.000
'cause if I could help Cincinnati

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
become more LGBTQ
friendly, that can spread.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.900
I wanted to make that change.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.810
I was dedicated to making
that change in the world.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.810
So Cincinnati was my
battle ground. (laughs)

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.010
At P&amp;G, sexual orientation was added

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:06.170
to the EEO policy toward the end of '92.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.840
- You know, that was a big
thing for P&amp;G at the time,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.270
given its conservative nature.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:12.500
- It was a big day.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.210
I read it and couldn't believe it.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:15.970
I honestly couldn't believe my eyes.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.360
I think I walked out a
second time to re-read it

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.763
to make sure it was real.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.530
- There's beginning to be that feeling

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.210
of we're gonna be free,
and boy, how wrong we were.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.370
Since it's not like, boom you've got

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.770
this anti-discrimination
thing, and now it's gonna be,

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.650
you know, Bambi at the still blue lake.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:33.860
It just doesn't work that way.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.810
- [Man] P&amp;G almost did that.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.320
They almost put sexual
orientation in the policy

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.970
as a bit of a legal thing.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:41.990
- [Susan] That's exactly right.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:43.610
It wasn't like they were planning

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.700
to do anything different
once they got the words in.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.970
There was no program or policy

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.890
that I can remember
that went with it, none.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.430
- The initial perception
was that it was a win.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.330
Yeah, but it was not.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:01.330
- It provided recognition to
employees that we existed.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.680
- It actually got worse during that time.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.350
When I would walk into
the ladies restroom,

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.000
most of the people would walk out.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.670
If I sat down at one of
the long lunch tables

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.130
they would get up and leave.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.110
- And then at the same time in Cincinnati

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.320
you get this referendum
that passed putting in

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.636
the so-called Article XII
in the city's constitution.

00:03:21.636 --> 00:03:24.243
- Article XII, oh God,
Article XII. (groans)

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.180
- [Woman] Issue three
represents discrimination,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.220
and we're not gonna tolerate

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.840
discrimination in our community.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.990
- Article XII prevented
protection for gay people

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.690
in employment, in housing
within the city of Cincinnati.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:43.083
- Specifically excluded gay
people as a protected class.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.510
- It seems like you're making
it up because it's so crazy.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.710
It said that city council was disallowed

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.400
from passing any legislation

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.680
that would protect gay
people from discrimination.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.150
Now, that's not how it was on the ballot.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.770
The people that put it on the
ballot control the language.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.120
They would have this laundry
list of hot-button terms.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.280
You can't create special
rights for gay people.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.430
You can't create quotas.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.930
- Really, having a
house is special rights?

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.470
Having a job and not feel
like they could just walk in

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.610
and say, we really don't like you.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:15.470
I actually, once I was
asked to describe to a group

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.630
of P&amp;G women from around the
world what it's like to be gay,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.830
what the experience is like.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:23.470
And the way I described it
was, it is to never know

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.890
when you're going to be hunted.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.000
And therefore you always
have to be prepared

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.670
that this is the day that somebody decided

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.533
that your wellbeing is a
threat to their happiness.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.540
- [Andi] Not only did article XII pass,

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.743
but 2/3 of the voters in
Cincinnati voted for it.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.880
- [Delaine] I remember walking
through the halls at P&amp;G

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.290
and thinking, the majority
of you voted for this.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.380
You went out of your way to make sure

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.570
that I don't feel secure
in my job or my housing.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.700
- I don't know that I was ever
as mad again as I was the day

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.687
after article 12 passed,
and I walked in and said,

00:05:03.687 --> 00:05:05.827
"I'm going to make change
happen in the halls

00:05:05.827 --> 00:05:08.500
"and walls of my employer."

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.180
- I started looking for my community,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:16.180
for LGBTQ people in P&amp;G
who I could relate to.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:21.056
And I ended up finding the pot lucks.

00:05:21.056 --> 00:05:22.650
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.410
- Enough of us had kind of
chatted and said hey, you know,

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.123
if this is something
that's recognized by P&amp;G,

00:05:29.123 --> 00:05:32.530
it's in our equal employment
opportunity policy,

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:34.470
we should feel free to associate

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.103
as a group of gay and lesbian employees.

00:05:39.483 --> 00:05:40.316
(whistle whistling)

00:05:40.316 --> 00:05:41.810
- What happened with
me is I was playing in

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.690
a volleyball league that happened
to have Gary Wright in it.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.057
And he said, "I sort of noticed
that you're blatantly out.

00:05:47.057 --> 00:05:49.840
"Do you have any interest in
being a part of this group?"

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.040
And I'm like, "absolutely."

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.210
I mean, it really was part of
my journey even before P&amp;G,

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.800
and that I wanted to
continue, of actively working

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.203
to create the world I want to live in.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.990
- I had just moved back to
Cincinnati with the family,

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:05.800
and it was difficult.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.833
So I wanted to have the
chance to make a difference.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.570
- Part of what Gary brought

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.770
was he could talk his way
around a lot of things.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.290
He was very comfortable with leadership.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.860
He was really good at that subtle stuff.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.130
That was one of his super powers.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.120
So five of us came together

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.100
to talk about what we
wanted from the company.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.773
That was the start of
everything was those meetings.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.820
- During that time article
XII emboldened even more

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:39.820
name calling and threats,
like you know, that posturing.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.840
I remember walking with
our director at P&amp;G,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.200
and we had passed a newspaper stand,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.430
and there was a headline
about gays wanting to,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.960
like petitioning to parade
or something like that.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.787
And he said, "Oh my God, those people

00:06:53.787 --> 00:06:55.121
"should all just go to an island."

00:06:55.121 --> 00:06:57.040
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.930
- If you're in the closet
you can't be bringing

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.190
all your energy to the
workplace because you're holding

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.120
some of that energy to
protect your own identity,

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.923
your own sense of safety, I think.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.060
- So the five of us got together.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.540
There was discussion around
the interest of saying hey,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.640
you know, this is great
to have this EEO policy,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.070
but there are actions in the workplace

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:21.470
that we need to address,
and there are further things

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.570
that can be done in deploying policy.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.020
First we need an identity.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.390
I mean, we work in a branding
company, let's get serious.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:33.970
- I started just playing
around with logos and names

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.410
so that we could come up with some way

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.720
to refer to us that wasn't necessarily

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.620
like flashing red lights, the gay group.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.850
- [Andi] So we started calling it GABLE.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:46.060
- Sort of the idea of the
gable of a house being a roof

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:50.030
and protection and a gathering
place over the people.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:52.040
So it had a little bit of meaning for us

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.990
as a word and a name.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.203
The letters reflected who we wanted to be.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.890
- [Susan] GABLE being formed
in the mid-90s was a big deal,

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.840
even for someone like me.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.853
I felt, maybe I'm not
totally alone anymore.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.250
- [Ed] There's a number
of what the company

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.990
was beginning to call diversity groups.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.650
You know, women managers,
or African Americans.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.120
- Affinity groups at Procter and Gamble

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.580
typically had an ION conference.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.060
And it stands for Inter Office Network.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.840
So that they could
communicate among one another.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.670
- So there was a normal
process for requesting

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:30.830
an ION conference for a group,
so I just filled out the form

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:33.460
and submitted it, and
we got our ION group.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.430
- The ION conference was
really a way of saying

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.490
we would be officially recognized.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.920
- We had it all going,
we were cooking with gas.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.660
And then (mimics knife
slicing) just got cut off.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.230
This is an inappropriate
use of company resources.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:46.230
Based on what?

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.350
I mean, the funny thing was

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:48.890
there was no process that existed.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.870
They had to create it to
say no to us, basically.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.930
But that's how it got to be
the significance that it was.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.350
It wasn't that to begin with,
it was just an ION conference.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.340
Everybody was a part of
like 18 ION conferences.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.120
But, I mean that's really
what led us to standing up

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.313
and saying we want to
formally ask for these things.

00:09:05.313 --> 00:09:07.463
- We want these three things, yeah.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.380
The three requests were
that, first we wanted gay

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.790
and lesbian issues included
in diversity training.

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:19.100
The second one was the ION conference.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.560
And then the third one was
domestic partner benefits.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.790
- Domestic partner benefits
was always the core

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:27.370
that held us together.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:28.853
It was of universal importance to us.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.190
- So what we decided was we'd start

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:34.903
at the very top of the company.

00:09:34.903 --> 00:09:35.880
(pages flipping)

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.470
We wanted to go to the top HR person.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.930
We sent him a letter and
requested a meeting with him

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.570
to talk about what we wanted.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:47.220
He sent us back a note basically saying,

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.780
I don't meet with groups like yours.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.860
I have my direct reports do that.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.477
- Come on now, cut me a break.

00:09:53.477 --> 00:09:54.310
(hand knocking on wood)

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:57.790
- What we realized was that he
had just given us permission

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:01.930
to approach every single one
of his direct reports and say,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.700
your boss said we're
supposed to meet with you.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:07.950
So we wrote LaVelle Bond and said,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.160
we're a group of P&amp;G employees.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.350
We're lesbian, gay, and bisexual.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.070
And we'd like to meet with
you to talk about that.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:17.640
He wrote back to us and said,

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.373
you can meet with my
associate VP, Waldo Jeff.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:26.350
- I wasn't in that organization a week

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.451
before I received a
call from Heidi Bruins.

00:10:29.451 --> 00:10:31.621
(phone ringing)

00:10:31.621 --> 00:10:34.930
I don't even know where the
bathroom is in this organization

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.260
yet and you're already
calling me to begin the battle

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.597
in the global world with
sexual orientation (laughs).

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:44.267
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:10:44.267 --> 00:10:46.740
- Waldo was there and he was writing

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.480
an agenda on the white board
and his hand was shaking.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.690
He was so nervous.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.683
- I really didn't know what to expect.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.090
- He might have been scared
of us that first meeting,

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:04.090
but he listened, and that was
really evident in who he was.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.370
- If you're being discriminated against

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.220
I have learned you have to fight back.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.953
I was born in segregated New Orleans.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:18.630
I have seen exclusion and discrimination

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.173
at a very early age.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:26.110
If gays and lesbians at
P&amp;G are willing to stand up

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:28.240
for themselves and fight for their rights,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.380
I sure know what it's like to do that.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.007
When this young lady, Heidi
Bruins, got in my ear and said,

00:11:36.007 --> 00:11:39.797
"Waldo, you know nobody else
is going to advocate for us

00:11:39.797 --> 00:11:44.797
"but you," I knew that she was right.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.870
- At P&amp;G the whole idea is
that you're sharing data.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.710
We're all about the data.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:57.480
- For over 160 years, Procter &amp; Gamble

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.360
has been selling its products
to gay and lesbian consumers,

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.160
but we haven't talked about it much.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.980
- We did the research, found
out what other companies

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.890
were doing, understood
the tax implications

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.880
and the costs that they had incurred.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:12.620
- We would have these binders

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.810
that would have a lot of information.

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:17.720
The kinds of questions that people ask.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:21.060
You know, how much is this gonna
cost, who does this affect,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.750
why should we do this, where
is the justification for it?

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:26.920
Just to help people to make
their decisions in our favor,

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.010
to side with us, was the idea.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:33.560
- It was pretty much a
resistance initially.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:37.610
One time one of the vice
presidents came into my office

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:42.610
and shut the door, and told
me that he was concerned that

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:49.180
I was beginning to create an
environment in the company

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:52.880
that was immoral and
he would never support,

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.370
and that my working with top
management on that had to cease

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:01.833
or he personally was gonna see
to it that I was terminated.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:07.680
What that incident taught me
was how we use power to bully.

00:13:10.397 --> 00:13:11.230
(crowd shouting)

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.490
- [Delaine] One of the
objections that we heard

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.510
is that you're telling us
about these things that happen

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.210
in these far off places.
Gay rights now!

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.170
- They don't happen in Cincinnati,

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.710
and they don't happen at P&amp;G.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:27.530
But because of Article
XII they actually did.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.348
So we started collecting people's stories.

00:13:30.348 --> 00:13:33.681
(warm electronic music)

00:13:36.975 --> 00:13:39.240
- I was so scared that
the powers at be at P&amp;G

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.890
would find out I was gay
for like, seven years.

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.950
In addition to lying by
omission about my lifestyle,

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:49.410
I actually manufactured
a wedding and a husband.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.700
That, of course, resulted in
a very tangled web of lies.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:56.100
But my energy was totally
focused on maintaining the lies.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.783
I had ulcers, paranoia, and
a total loss of self-esteem.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.450
- One time they were in the throes

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:08.900
of doing some mandatory training,
and there was a manager,

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:12.307
and he said, "If I ever see any gay people

00:14:12.307 --> 00:14:13.607
"I'm just gonna kill 'em."

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.080
- I had had an international assignment.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:20.350
I'd lived for three years in
Manila in the Philippines,

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.320
and my partner was there with me.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.040
We had to figure out how to have him

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:25.300
be there living with me.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:28.400
There was no official accommodation

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.637
for a partner at that point in time.

00:14:30.637 --> 00:14:33.760
I mean, this was incredibly difficult.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:37.750
- I was pretty proud of
my family, my three sons

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.640
and the life we had built together.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.627
I never felt like my company
recognized my family.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:49.880
That was the emotional toll.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.440
Financially it was oppressive

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:54.240
because I couldn't get insurance

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.010
for two of the three
children and my partner.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:02.100
We had to purchase insurance outside,

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:03.780
and that was extremely expensive.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:07.773
Probably $55,000 just in
medical care for my family.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:11.200
- And so what we did is we sent two people

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:15.830
to meet with every single HR
leader, and whenever they met

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:19.400
with us they were like, oh,
you're like normal people.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.293
You look just like us.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.870
- And then we'd share our stories.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.630
And that was the big thing

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:31.550
that made a difference for so many people.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.410
- You know, it was the
difference between people,

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:35.700
like, I'm agreeing with
this thing over here,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.520
or I'm disagreeing with
this thing over here.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:41.563
And it would bring it into, I
am looking at a real person.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.310
- So this is where we
went to Charlotte Otto,

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.120
and public relations, so that
you could look at it and say

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:52.810
okay, can the company manage
the external communication

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.159
of this without getting beat up?

00:15:55.159 --> 00:15:59.870
- I think sharing his personal
stories really took it

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:02.580
from the intellectual, this is right

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.830
for the business, to, this is

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:10.830
right for our people because
we care about every individual.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:16.620
Because I got a personal understanding

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:20.999
that took me from observer to advocate.

00:16:20.999 --> 00:16:24.350
(warm instrumental music)

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.890
- Her role wasn't to say
we should go do this,

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.630
her role was to say, I can manage this.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.610
Here was the person in
charge of the function

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.630
that was responsible for making sure

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:36.210
that the external
communication wouldn't blow up

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.460
in the company's face
saying, we can manage this.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:40.693
- And at that point we had been working

00:16:40.693 --> 00:16:42.890
on this for many, many years.

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:45.610
- It was a very long process.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.130
- Over the course of five years

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.120
we had prepared the environment

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:55.120
to be able to accept
another level of thinking.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:57.680
And at that point we felt

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:02.050
that we were pretty much
ready to move forward.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:05.413
So we got the company to
vote on same-sex benefits.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.893
It was an overwhelming vote of a yes.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:16.233
And unfortunately, the
vote was not implemented.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:22.250
- The answer came back
of a very disappointing,

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.530
we won't be leaders.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.790
We will continue to benchmark,
and we will do this mid pack.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:30.250
That was the exact language
used with this, mid pack.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.293
We felt defeated.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:34.710
- It became apparent that
through the grapevine,

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:38.170
that they were going to be
offering insurance for pets.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.390
And I thought I was gonna die.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:45.160
There's no way my company
can make a mistake like that.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.716
To cover pets and not cover children.

00:17:48.716 --> 00:17:52.216
(soft instrumental music)

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:57.590
- [Susan] Back then I was

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.770
the one senior manager who was out.

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.160
There were people who did
a lot more for gay rights

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.210
at Procter who really worked
it, worked it so hard.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.680
And I can't say that I did.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.390
I just was trying to stay
alive most of the time.

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:12.910
I wasn't looking for people's approval.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.710
I was just looking for acceptance.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:17.800
All I want is a level playing field.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:21.863
Let me do good work and judge me fairly.

00:18:21.863 --> 00:18:23.232
(soft instrumental music)

00:18:23.232 --> 00:18:26.100
- I remember I got a call from someone

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.580
who at that time I didn't know.

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:30.750
And his name was Gary Wright,
and he came to my office

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.730
as he said he wanted to do

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.600
to talk about a subject of diversity.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:37.300
And he came and he had a couple of folks

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:41.380
with him who I can hardly remember.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.600
But I certainly remember Gary.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:46.237
He said, "John, I know you're
very committed to diversity.

00:18:46.237 --> 00:18:49.807
"That's obvious to us
all, but you need to know

00:18:49.807 --> 00:18:51.617
"that there is a huge outage in the way

00:18:51.617 --> 00:18:54.670
"you are approaching
diversity in this company."

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:56.920
Well, that got my attention.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:59.190
- And what he said he was talking
about was that we were not

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:03.040
recognizing the different
way of life of our employees.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.750
Now, Susan Arnold was
a very important person

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.340
to me with the company.

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:08.760
Susan and I had worked together

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:13.140
in different parts of the
soap business for years.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.450
And I never really
thought about her marital

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:19.160
or her personal life one way or the other.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.670
And I really hadn't sat
down with her and had a,

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.620
kind of conversation that, who are you,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.040
what's your whole life like?

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.850
- Well, there was one
of these dividend days,

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:33.080
and I saw Susan across the
way, and she was sitting there

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:35.810
with two young children and her partner.

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:37.530
It came together for
me in a particular way

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:41.350
because I knew Susan in so
many different dimensions,

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:42.713
but I hadn't seen this.

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:47.610
It was the importance of
recognizing a person's full life

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:49.170
and the importance of
their having a choice

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:50.520
on what that life would be.

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:54.767
- [Susan] A.G. Lafley called,
and he said to me, "Susan,

00:19:54.767 --> 00:19:57.687
"I promise you that we are going

00:19:57.687 --> 00:20:00.373
"to get domestic partner benefits."

00:20:00.373 --> 00:20:04.040
(dramatic electronic music)

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:10.560
- For P&amp;G, and for me, diversity
is a business strategy.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:14.970
It's not just about doing the right thing,

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.480
it's about doing right things right.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.630
And it's about building our business

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:22.413
and our organization for the long-term.

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:26.780
- That that point having
a company like P&amp;G

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:31.170
make a bold statement like
that was incredibly powerful.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.810
- [Susan] When Procter &amp; Gamble did it

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.070
it said something, it spoke volumes.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:39.130
It risked company boycott,
it risked product boycott.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:42.600
It wasn't, it just wasn't
that they did it right,

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.380
they did it right and they did
it in the face of opposition.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:50.100
- [Dick] Focus on the
Family decided to declare

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:52.610
a nationwide boycott of our products,

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:53.810
and we had to go to the board

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:55.530
because it did impact our business,

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:56.930
and they were supportive.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.763
I mean, they didn't just back down at all.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:04.490
We may not have been first
to the party, but we have,

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.190
I think threw a pretty good party.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:10.870
- Same-sex partner benefits
gave us the courage

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:14.232
to step out even more,
to take on Article XII.

00:21:14.232 --> 00:21:16.300
(stirring instrumental music)

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:18.740
But it's each of those
steps that have given us

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:21.680
a stronger motivation and a stronger voice

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:23.613
to be a positive force of change.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.210
- [Charlotte] Support for the repeal

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:30.140
was the point at which
P&amp;G became more public

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:35.140
about its commitment to equal
treatment for LGBT people.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:36.900
It was the right thing to do.

00:21:37.856 --> 00:21:40.100
- I don't know that
anybody had a master plan

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.210
for, let's do this slowly,
and then this slowly,

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:45.760
and this slowly, and keep
that forward momentum.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.920
But what I think it says
is there's a process

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:53.920
we tend to follow, and we
may not get somewhere first,

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:58.140
but we'll get there better
and ultimately it sticks.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:21:59.810
- You look at the dramatic change

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:02.420
of the original EEO policy,

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:04.930
you look at the public announcement

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:07.580
of the final domestic partner benefits,

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:12.580
and you have the support for
the repeal of Article XII.

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:15.590
Those three were big changes

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:17.960
over a fairly long period of time.

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:20.770
Maybe over a 12-year period or so.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:24.460
But they were big
changes, each one of them.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:27.420
- I think the thing that I'm proud of

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:30.130
is if people don't really
want to think about being gay,

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:31.530
if there's other things that they are,

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:34.180
like a woman scientist,
or just a woman in general

00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:36.240
who's like a strong leader,

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.203
they get to focus on those things.

00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:42.300
That's really the win for
me is that people don't have

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.950
to think about it if they don't
want to, and they can focus

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:50.790
on the parts of themselves
that they do want to focus on.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:55.440
- To hear that GABLE
is 5,000 members strong

00:22:55.440 --> 00:23:00.233
and in 54 countries makes me want to cry.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.700
I think back when there were, (chuckles)

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:08.373
there were just six of us.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:12.510
I don't know that I
ever would have imagined

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.163
a day like this would come.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:16.980
- We look just like you
and we talk just like you,

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:18.863
and we are human just like you.

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:21.810
Really, there's gay and lesbian people

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.040
all around you here (laughs).

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:27.893
- This is my poem that I
wrote for my retirement.

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:33.350
As a youngster growing up in
the bowels of the deep South,

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:35.990
I had the passion for equality,

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:38.313
and of course this big, big mouth.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:44.150
Expected to be treated equal,
equality had such huge appeal.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:52.033
All I ever really wanted
was a level playing field.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:56.890
Those of you trying to become
the best that you can be,

00:23:56.890 --> 00:24:01.493
use my experience at P&amp;G
as one last gift from me.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:06.923
Give more than you take,
perform with passion and zeal,

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:13.320
demand excellence and equality,
and a level playing field.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:17.190
(paper flapping)

